;; The verified core of this port's exit status.
;;
;; TOOL-ORACLE-1: this port had NO contract at all, so `--strict-verified-core`
;; decided nothing about it and its only instrument was a cover whose reference
;; is deleted. What survived was the port agreeing with itself.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MODULE. A `def` cannot call a sibling `def`: the failure
;; is at check time, before verification, and no ordering escapes it. Cross-module
;; calls to an already-verified callee do work, leaf-first. The split is forced by
;; the language and the port imports this. Same shape as tools/version-gate/,
;; tools/doc-path-lint/ and tools/build-smoke/.
;;
;; WHAT IS NOT PROVED HERE, said plainly so the file is not mistaken for more
;; than it is. Every recognizer in the port is a bool- or string-valued body over
;; string comparison and falls to erBodyFallback (STRLIT-BODY-1). The port's
;; ORDER of checks is not proved either. What is proved is the exit status.
;;
;; THE RANGE IS WHAT STOPS A SILENT SUCCESS. POSIX truncates a status to the low
;; 8 bits, so an unclamped 256 exits 0 and reports a PASS over a rejected tree.
;; The three implications pin the policy and not merely the range, because a body
;; clamping the wrong way satisfies the range and still fails open.
;;
;; MEASURED at v0.16.2: verifies SAFE, reports `body-faithful: status-of`. The
;; refuting case is committed as crux-status-unclamped.llmll and asserted to FAIL.

(module adjudicate

  ;; `c` is the code the console loop carries out of its Done state. The port
  ;; projects it and passes it here as a scalar: a `:status` function is a
  ;; strict-core `def` and may call only body-faithful functions, while the
  ;; port's own projection is a `def-shell` over a composite state and falls
  ;; back (PROC-BOUNDARY-1). Taking an int keeps the range obligation PROVED.
  (def status-of [c: int] -> int
    (post (and (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
               (and (=> (< c 0) (= result 1))
                    (and (=> (> c 255) (= result 255))
                         (=> (and (>= c 0) (<= c 255)) (= result c))))))
    (if (< c 0) 1 (if (> c 255) 255 c))))
